// shader-differential — NAMED REGRESSION for the LANE-EXTRACT half of the
// scalar-broadcast rule (t_b6f2a2a4, companion to sd_scalar_broadcast.fcg).
//
// THE RULE: a scalar operand under a multi-lane write mask must replicate
// ITS OWN component, not lane 0.  A width-1 value can be a lane extract
// that has already selected its component - `uv.y` carries swizzle[0]==1 -
// so forcing {0,0,0,0} would silently read lane X instead, turning the fix
// into the same class of bug it removes.
//
// WHY A SEPARATE FIXTURE, stated accurately after MEASURING it rather than
// from the claim I first wrote here.  I originally asserted that
// sd_scalar_broadcast could not catch a replicate-vs-force-lane-0 mistake.
// That was FALSE: mutating broadcastScalar to force lane 0 changes its
// container by 4 bytes, because its output assembly reads `frac(lit).y`
// and friends - lane extracts with swizzle[0] == 1 - so it catches the
// mutation incidentally through its RESULT reads.
//
// This fixture is still worth having, for narrower reasons than I claimed:
// it exercises the lane extract as the SCALAR OPERAND of a multi-lane
// operation (which is where the rule applies) rather than as an output
// selector, and it covers lanes Z and W of a uniform, which nothing else
// touches.  It detects the same mutation with 8 bytes against 4.
//
// The correction matters more than the fixture: a docstring asserting what
// a test protects is not evidence that it protects it, and I wrote a false
// one while relaying exactly that lesson from the vita-cg-compiler team,
// who had just found their own fixture testing a different mechanism than
// its docstring claimed.  Both fixtures are now mutation-confirmed against
// force-lane-0; neither claim rests on reading.
//
// EACH CHANNEL TAKES ITS SCALAR FROM A DIFFERENT NON-X LANE, so a failure
// names which lane was lost rather than only that something was:
//   R  scalar from uv.y      under a 2-lane mask, read back at lane Y
//   G  scalar from a uniform's .z lane, under a 2-lane mask, at lane Y
//   B  scalar from a uniform's .w lane, under a 2-lane mask, at lane Y
// If the broadcast forces lane 0, R reads uv.x where it should read uv.y
// and G/B read u_lane.x - all three go wrong together and none goes to
// zero, which is the signature that distinguishes this from the
// read-zero and read-unwritten-lane failures of the sibling fixture.
//
// Non-flat input on purpose: judged on pixels, because a broadcast that is
// present in the encoding but wrong in the swizzle reads identically in a
// disassembly listing.

void main(float2 uv : TEXCOORD0,
          uniform float4 u_lane,
          out float4 color : COLOR)
{
    float  sy = uv.y;                   // scalar taken from lane Y
    float2 a  = uv * sy;

    float  sz = u_lane.z;               // scalar taken from a uniform's lane Z
    float2 b  = uv * sz;

    float  sw = u_lane.w;               // scalar taken from a uniform's lane W
    float2 c  = uv * sw;

    color = float4(frac(a).y, frac(b).y, frac(c).y, 1.0);
}
